The block distinguishes calendar capability, adequate service during positive demand and delivered work; first-failure survival, uninterrupted mission and restored endpoint availability; incomplete observation and actual failure; shared supports and independent complete paths; finite observed time fractions and conditional limiting cycle averages. The original twelve-hour log gives 5/6 calendar capability, 4/9 demand-time availability and 1/2 requested work delivered. It explicitly assumes demand cannot queue. Its implementation validates a complete interval partition before reusing the existing clipped-window helper. Undefined demand ratios remain undefined.

The common-support example derives service probability 0.864 and the 0.0576 error from treating the overlapping paths as independent. The cycle-average argument derives u/(u+d) from convergent finite sample averages and bounds the incomplete final cycle; it does not infer stable means from a short log. Waiting, access, diagnosis and return evidence belong to restoration time when they prevent the required service. First-failure hazard, exposure likelihood and zero-failure limits have explicit model/observation premises.

An original three-component model represents two units, a common support and one repair crew. Failures apply per calendar slot to components that are up at its start, including units waiting for support; only an already-down component can be selected for repair. Changes take effect at the slot end. The derivations establish transition normalization, distribution propagation, expected delivered output and uninterrupted-mission mass. Event order, required capacity, endpoint timing, perfect state observation, available spares and immediate verified restoration are assumptions to criticize. The example compares two fixed repair priorities without claiming global optimality. Its changed-model assignment requires a substantive extension, independent evidence, criticism and revision. No real commissioning, learner performance or measured lifetime is claimed.

Existing `@met-handbook` and `@mech-nasa-systems` source identities gained bounded inspection notes. Actual NIST sections 8.1.3.1, 8.1.6.1 and 8.4.5.1 were inspected. The new study reconstructs the hypothetical 800-hour zero-failure example: 800/[−ln(0.05)] = 267.046560556267… hours. The final source example omits the minus sign even though the earlier formula and rounded positive result establish the intended calculation; the lesson states the correction. This is a published-calculation reconstruction, not raw-data access or a full confidence-table audit. Selected NASA 5.5 transition input/preparation passages were inspected for product identity, supporting resources and readiness. Existing edition/rights qualifications and historical inspections remain. Full source assignments and specialist mechanical procedures retain their scope; no new source identity or media was added.

